  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday morning through next Friday)\nIssued at 230 PM EDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nThe foremost headline of the coming week will be the building heat \nas an expansive upper ridge envelops the Southeast. Temperatures \nwill creep upward a degree or two from Monday-Wednesday with highs \nin the mid 90s commonplace by midweek. Maximum heat index values \nwill rise hand-in-hand, likely in the 100-105 range for most areas \noutside of the mountains. In any event, there are no hopes for \nnoticeably cooler air in the near future for those looking for any \nsigns of an end to summer.\n\nAs far as rain chances are concerned, PoPs will generally be \ntrending downward as the ridge expands eastward, likely on the order \nof isolated coverage at best through midweek. With that said, \nslightly higher coverage of diurnal convection is possible along the \nsouthern tier as deeper moisture characterized by PWATS 2+\" advects \nwestward within the easterly flow beneath the core of the ridge to \nthe north. \n\nThe forecast by the end of the week will then depend to \nsome degree on the track/evolution of the remnant wave of former \nTropical Storm Dolly as it pushes westward toward Florida and \npotentially the Gulf. Ensemble spread (and therefore forecast \nuncertainty) is high at this extended time frame regarding track or \nany further development; however, we'll continue to monitor for any \npotential outcome that could increase moisture and rainfall coverage \nby the end of forecast period.",
